INT: And was it a particularly Jewish high school?

SK: Not particularly. But there were a lot of Jewish students, because it seemed to attract--

I don't know. Jews have always been very, very committed to getting the best education they could have. So a lot applied for admission to that school, or actually they had exams. And it wasn't just that school. There were several schools that had admissions requirements, admission tests. So if you couldn't get into one school, you could still apply to one of the other testing schools. And I think we must have had maybe, I don't even know how many, what percentage, but there were quite a few. And a lot of the teachers were Jewish.

INT: Do you think the teachers knew your background or a lot of your-- and a lot of your classmates' backgrounds?

