INT: Did you go to college after high school?

SK: I did. I won a scholarship, a New York Regents scholarship that allowed me to use the money at a New York School. And I applied-- I applied and went to Cornell University. And my uncle Robert paid for me to go to school, whatever the difference was between what the scholarship provided and the tuition. But of course, tuition was nothing like what it is these days. I think it was $250 or something for a semester. But anyway, he not only paid the tuition, but the room-- you know, the room and board that was separate. But anyway, I went to-- I started when I applied to college, I applied to the School of Chemical Engineering. And I went there for one year. There were only three girls in the whole class. And all three of us switched out afterwards and went to other-- other parts of the university. But I remember the first day we-- one of the first days when they greeted all the students, they said, look to your right and look to your left. Two of you will not be here at the end of the four years. And they were right. It wasn't just the women, the men, too.

