INT: Did you practice the religion?

SK: Religion? Not when I was in college. I don't think they had a Hillel, but I don't really know. I think I went home when-- you know, during the holidays. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] But I think I was always very committed to the fact that I was Jewish and I was lucky to be alive. And it was one of my responsibilities to stay Jewish, because I was a lucky one to survive. So many people were killed. In fact, when the war started in Ukraine and I would watch the news, I would sit there and tears would run down my face, because I couldn't believe that we were going through this all over again, having survived something like that. But anyway, here it is years later and it's still going on. And it's worse because Israel is having its problems. [PAUSES] I've become addicted to the news. My daughter keeps telling me, turn it off and don't watch it. But you can't help it.

INT: Why are you addicted?

SK: Why am I addicted? Because as a Jew, I identify with what's going on, as a Jew and a survivor of the Holocaust I identify with people who are being oppressed and the major religion is pushing them-- is suppressing their feelings and their commitments. And I find it terrible. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] Especially now with the increased antisemitism that I couldn't imagine when I was younger. I just couldn't imagine it. I knew that there was antisemitism under the surface. But I couldn't imagine what's going on now.

INT: Did you experience direct or indirect antisemitism when you were in school or in college?

SK: No, I didn't. I didn't really. I sort of-- I don't think-- well, the only ones who knew that I was Jewish were my sorority sisters. The others, maybe they knew I was. I don't know. I don't remember. I don't remember experiencing anything. I did know that there were Jewish sororities and Christian ones, but I figured I joined the Christian one because I figured they should get to know a Jew. So since they invited me to join, I decided I would try it. And I became good friends with a number of them. I still have a couple that I keep in touch with once in a while.

INT: So did you tell them that you're a Holocaust survivor?

SK: No. I never talked about it.

INT: Why not?

SK: I just found it painful. I never, in fact, I didn't even with my children, I didn't talk about it. And they never brought it up-- seldom brought it up, because they knew I was so sensitive to it. Because every time I would say something, tears would come running down my face. Not that I-- not that I experienced a lot of suffering. But I was very aware of the fact that I didn't have a mother and I didn't-- I lost my brother, and my family was demolished, and so on. And that in itself made me-- made the tears come all the time.

INT: And what about your father? Did you talk with him about It?

SK: Some, some. That's how I learned most of whatever I knew. But I knew it was difficult for him to talk about it. [PAUSES] It's interesting. I mean, there was no question about being Jewish. But he was never very observant or anything like that. But I remember when he used to come to visit me. He would want to go to temple, you know, on Shabbat. But he didn't want to-- he didn't like. I belonged to a reform temple and it didn't mean it too much to him. So he liked going to either an Orthodox or conservative. So I used to drive him. And then I had to stop two blocks away so he could walk to Temple. Because he didn't want them to see him driving. He didn't mind me driving him, but he didn't want them to see that. But anyway.

Did I show you the book? Should I go get the book or not?

