SK: I think once he got married, he sort of put it behind him to some extent and lived in the present. He was working at the newspaper. And I was going to school. And my stepmother was in charge of the, you know, the house. [PAUSES] And I think he kept whatever was in there inside, didn't really talk about it. It was only after she passed away when he used to come to visit me that we would talk a little bit about what happened, and the story about how I was smuggled out of the ghetto. And the story about when he came to get me from Steffa.

Those things he-- I really didn't know those when I was young. I only learned about it later.

INT: Did he ever mention anything more about the ghetto, the resistance there, the underground work?

SK: What's that?

