SK: Well, I graduated college. And I was applying for jobs. And those were the days when they offered one thing to men and they offered something else to women. So I remember applying to a company called Union Carbide. And they offered me a job, but they told me how much they were going to pay me. And I said-- I said, I know you don't offer that to the men. Why are you offering it to me? And they said, because we don't-- you can't do the work that a man can do. I said, what kind of work? And they said, well, you-- you're not strong and so on. I said, is there weightlifting? You know, is there something I have to lift? No, but that-- that was their policy. So I ended up getting a job at Polaroid, which was paying a lot more. And I could-- I worked on some research projects there.

And then in later years, not in later years, but along the way, I was always interested in math, and I became interested in the stock market. So-- [PAUSES] in later years after I had children, I went back to school and studied for a CPA [PAUSES] at Bentley College. And I got my master's there. [PAUSES] And then I sat for the CPA exam, which I passed. So I worked at that for a little while. That was actually before the children were born. No, no. I guess it was after.

And I'm trying to think. I worked at Polaroid before my children were born doing research. And then after the children were at a certain age, I went back to school to get the CPA. [PAUSES] Math was always my strength, so I sort of went in that direction.

