SK: We were married in 1961, I believe. And my daughter was born a year later. And my son was born three years, two years after my daughter.

INT: What are their names?

SK: My daughter is Gwen. Gwen, actually, her-- her legal name is Gwendolyn Freda Kane. They call-- we all call her Gwen. But she was named after my mother, Freida Golda. So we took the initials and moved them around a little. But that's who she was named. And my son is Jonathan Kane, Jonathan Thomas Kane. And I think the Thomas was for [? Tuvi, ?] which was my father's, somebody on my father-- well, could have been [? Tevel. ?] I don't know. I'm not sure. But he had an influence on that. And my son lives in Connecticut. My daughter lives about two miles from here, maybe a little more. No, she's two miles from my old house, so she must be a good 3, 3 and 1/2 miles from here.

INT: Did your children get married?

SK: Yes. Both children are married. My daughter is celebrating her 40th anniversary this year, and she has three children, all very accomplished, if I might say. And--

INT: Can you tell us the name of your grandchildren?

SK: Sure. My daughter's three children are Emily, who's a lawyer in New York.

She-- what do they call it? She worked-- during her training, she worked for an appellate judge. And she worked in the Southern District of New York working for a judge. What do they call it? All of a sudden, I've gone blank. She was-- I can't believe it. Anyway, she had some very good training and she's working as a judge, as a lawyer. And her husband's a lawyer also. She's married. Then my, the middle child, my daughter's middle child is Daniel, and he's working in real estate, working very long hours. And he's married also. And he's the one who lived in Cambridge and just moved to Belmont. And the youngest one is Ben, who arranged with you for me to have this interview. And Ben has done all kinds of things, including played baseball in the Olympics for the Israeli team. And the one who caught that baseball that he gave me as a present when his team beat Germany. Because he knew it would have special significance for me. [PAUSES] And then my son, who is now in-- he's-- I think he's going to be interviewed somewhere along the way. He is John. We call him John. He's in the-- in the American Virgin Islands today. He's flying home tonight. His son is working there, so he went to visit. And he has two children. His son, George, who is working in the hospitality industry, they call it, with the fancy hotels.

