INT: So you mentioned that this interview was organized with Ben, one of your grandchildren. [PAUSES] When did you first tell them about your experiences? Because you said to your children you rarely or hardly talk about it. What about with your grandchildren?

SK: I think it started when we-- the whole family would get together for Passover. And so during the Passover, when my father was alive, he would come to the Passover Seder. And he always read something about the Holocaust as part of the ceremony. And when he passed away, I started doing that. And so I would say something about the Holocaust during Passover. And once in a while, I would add something more. But I didn't talk about it very much. But they all knew. I don't know whether they knew from my father or from me. I-- just one of those.

INT: So your grandchildren knew your father?

SK: What's that?

INT: Your grandchildren knew your father?

SK: Definitely, Yes. My father used to, both my father and my stepmother used to come to visit and they would come like for a week and spend time, you know, time with us, and get to know the children. They both enjoyed the children a lot. And then after she passed away, my father would come and visit, you know, a few times a year.

INT: And when did your father pass away?

SK: I think it was 1983. I'm not sure. I have it written down. But he was-- he was-- yeah, he was, I think, 83 years old when he passed away. I have his obituary printed up in on one of the papers that I have that tells about his Holocaust experience, a little bit about his Holocaust experience. He went through a lot. He-- a wall fell on him, and he had-- he broke a hip. And couldn't be treated because he didn't-- you know, it was during the war. And somehow he survived. I don't know. It healed, didn't heal properly, but it healed. And [PAUSES] he once told me about a death march that he was on. And they were marching them to someplace where they were shooting them and killing them. And there was a Nazi soldier with a gun walking alongside this column of Jews. And a little girl came over to talk to the soldier, and the soldier bent down to talk. And my father ran off into the woods, and was able to escape.

That's something he told me about. I wasn't there and I could only-- I can only believe what he said. Why would he make it up if it wasn't true? But anyway.

INT: Do you know how long he hid in the woods afterwards?

SK: I don't really know. He must have found some other people to join and be with. He couldn't have survived all by himself. But [PAUSES] I don't really know. I don't even know what year that happened. All I know is that he wrote down on that calendar I showed you that that was-- 1943 was when my mother and brother disappeared. And he considered that the time that they died. So when I-- when I commemorate their yahrtzeit, that's the date I use, because he used that. But I don't know how long. Sometime between that and the end of the war, I guess. I don't know.

INT: Did your father ever share his story with Holocaust related?

SK: No, no. We used to go. He would go to-- he belonged to a-- what do they call it? [PAUSES] You know, an immigrant society that he would go, would meet with, and they would get together and, you know, talk and have dinner or whatever. So he did that, but he never went with any official group. Although when we were in Florida-- I'm trying to remember. [PAUSES 5 SECONDS] No, he never went to the one-- I went to one in Florida, a group that got together, a number of them were Dachau survivors. And I got to know, you know, a number of them. But my father never did that. But he did belong to some groups in the Bronx when he lived there.

INT: What sort of groups?

SK: They were-- they were European people who-- immigrants who came here, and they sort of found each other.

INT: Did he ever go back to Europe?

SK: He never did, no.

INT: You?

SK: I've been to Europe. Yeah, one time I even flew a German airline, and I said-- I always said I would never fly. I never bought German cars. I never bought-- I never wanted to fly German anything. But we went on a trip with friends. And they made the reservation and we went on the German airline. And don't you know it? They bumped us.

INT: Two?

SK: Us and they kept all the German passengers, but they bumped the Americans. [PAUSES] I think they had trouble with the plane, and they got another plane. But they only took the German passengers. They bumped the Americans. And luckily, we were able to get flights on American Airlines. And we were very relieved. And I think I wrote them three or four letters complaining about the fact that they didn't, you know, transport us the way they had said they would. [PAUSES] I think I got one letter back saying, thank you for your letter. That was it.

INT: And did you go back to Lithuania?

